## First In, First Out

A **queue** is a FIFO (First In, First Out) data structure. New items join at the back, and items leave from the front — like a line at a coffee shop.

### Building a Queue from a Vec

```xiom
type Queue[T] = {
  items: Vec[T];
}

fn Queue.new[T]() -> Queue[T] {
  return Queue[T]{ items: Vec[T].new() };
}

fn Queue.enqueue[T](self: &mut Queue[T], item: T) {
  self.items.push(item);
}
```

Enqueue is easy — just push to the end. But dequeue needs to remove the FIRST item:

### Dequeue: Remove from Front

```xiom
fn Queue.dequeue[T](self: &mut Queue[T]) -> Option[T] {
  if self.items.len() == 0 {
    return None;
  }

  // Get the first item
  let front = match self.items.get(0) { Some(v) => v, None => return None };

  // Rebuild the Vec without the first item
  let new_items: Vec[T] = Vec[T].new();
  let i = 1;
  while i < self.items.len() {
    let item = match self.items.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };
    new_items.push(item);
    i = i + 1;
  }

  // Replace the old Vec
  while self.items.len() > 0 {
    self.items.pop();
  }
  i = 0;
  while i < new_items.len() {
    let item = match new_items.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => "" };
    self.items.push(item);
    i = i + 1;
  }

  return Some(front);
}
```

### Peek: Look Without Removing

```xiom
fn Queue.peek[T](self) -> Option[T] {
  return self.items.get(0);
}

fn Queue.is_empty[T](self) -> Bool {
  return self.items.len() == 0;
}

fn Queue.len[T](self) -> Int {
  return self.items.len();
}
```

### Using the Queue

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var q: Queue[Str] = Queue[Str].new();

  q.enqueue("Print job #1");
  q.enqueue("Print job #2");
  q.enqueue("Print job #3");

  io.println(string.int_to_str(q.len()));

  let next = q.dequeue();
  io.println(match next { Some(j) => j, None => "empty" });
  io.println(string.int_to_str(q.len()));
  return 0;
}
```

### The FIFO Guarantee

Enqueuing A, B, C then dequeuing three times ALWAYS returns A, then B, then C — in the exact order they arrived.

